QUESTIONS = [
    # CH48 L2 remaining (+1)
    {"text":"Consider the following about ECI and constitutional amendments:\n1. Parliament can restrict ECI's powers through law, but cannot abolish ECI without a constitutional amendment.\n2. ECI cannot be merged with any other constitutional body without a constitutional amendment.\n3. The President can by executive order significantly curtail ECI's operational powers.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — executive orders cannot curtail powers conferred by Article 324 (a constitutional provision). Statements 1 and 2 are correct — ECI cannot be abolished or merged without amending Article 324.","chapter_number":48,"level":2},

    # CH48 L3 (30)
    {"text":"Consider the following about the constitutional basis of ECI's power to issue guidelines to political parties:\n1. ECI's power to issue guidelines to parties derives exclusively from the RPA 1951.\n2. ECI can issue binding guidelines to parties under its plenary powers from Article 324.\n3. Political parties that violate ECI guidelines can be derecognised.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect — ECI's power over political parties is primarily derived from Article 324's plenary powers and the Election Symbols Order, not exclusively the RPA. Statements 2 and 3 are correct.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the Supreme Court's interpretation of Article 324's scope in Mohinder Singh Gill v. CEC (1978):\n1. The SC held that ECI's powers under Article 324 are not confined to what statutes expressly authorise.\n2. Article 324 is a 'plenary' provision — ECI can regulate elections even in areas statute is silent.\n3. Mohinder Singh Gill specifically struck down the ECI's power to countermand elections.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — the Mohinder Singh Gill case affirmed ECI's broad powers; it did not strike down the power to countermand. Statements 1 and 2 correctly capture the landmark ruling.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the 'right to reject' (NOTA) and its significance:\n1. NOTA gives voters the option to reject all candidates while maintaining the secrecy of ballot.\n2. If NOTA gets the highest number of votes in a constituency, a by-election is held.\n3. NOTA votes are not counted as valid votes for determining winning candidate's margin.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect — currently, under Indian law, if NOTA gets the highest votes, the candidate with the next highest votes wins; there is no re-election (though the SC has been petitioned on this). Statements 1 and 3 are correct.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the Electoral Bond Scheme (struck down in 2024):\n1. The scheme allowed companies and individuals to buy bonds from SBI and donate them anonymously to political parties.\n2. The SC held this violated the right to information under Article 19(1)(a) — voters have the right to know political funding.\n3. The SC held that political parties also have a fundamental right to receive anonymous donations.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — the SC rejected the argument that parties have a fundamental right to anonymous donations; it held voters' right to information was paramount. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the CEC appointment process after the Chief Election Commissioners (Amendment) Act, 2023:\n1. The selection panel includes the PM, a Cabinet Minister, and the Leader of Opposition.\n2. The SC had earlier in Anoop Baranwal v. Union of India (2023) directed that CEC appointments must include the CJI until Parliament legislates.\n3. The 2023 Act was enacted in direct response to the SC ruling in Anoop Baranwal to exclude the judiciary from the selection panel.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct. The 2023 Act responded to Anoop Baranwal (2023) which included the CJI in an interim selection committee. The Act then explicitly excluded the CJI, substituting a Cabinet Minister.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the relationship between ECI independence and democratic backsliding:\n1. Comparative constitutional scholars monitor ECI's independence as an indicator of India's democratic health.\n2. ECI's removal of the CJI from its appointment committee (2023 Act) has been criticised by election monitoring bodies.\n3. V-Dem and Freedom House have both downgraded India's electoral democracy status citing ECI independence concerns.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three reflect documented reports from comparative democracy monitoring organisations. V-Dem's Electoral Democracy Index and Freedom House's India ratings both cite ECI independence as a concern.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about ECI and multi-phase elections:\n1. Multi-phase elections are used to ensure adequate security force deployment across large states.\n2. ECI can split polling across phases in a state to better deploy CAPF.\n3. Multi-phase elections violate equal treatment of candidates as MCC applies differently in early vs late phases.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is a debated criticism but not a settled constitutional violation — MCC applies uniformly from the date of schedule announcement to the end of counting. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about 'electoral autocracy' risks and constitutional design:\n1. The founding framers designed ECI with constitutional status specifically to prevent majoritarian governments from controlling elections.\n2. The SC's Anoop Baranwal ruling specifically cited the risk of electoral autocracy as a reason to include the CJI in the selection panel.\n3. Constitutional independence alone is sufficient to prevent electoral manipulation — institutional culture matters less.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — institutional culture, norms, and leadership are critical; constitutional text alone is insufficient. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about 'money power' in Indian elections:\n1. India has some of the highest election spending per vote among democracies.\n2. The disclosure requirements under ECI make all election expenditure fully transparent.\n3. The disconnect between declared expenditure and actual spending remains a major challenge for ECI.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect — disclosure requirements have significant gaps; black money, benami donations, and expenditure by third parties (outside candidates' accounts) remain poorly tracked. Statements 1 and 3 are correct.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the SC's approach to electoral reforms in India:\n1. SC has issued many electoral reform orders including criminal disclosure (2002), NOTA (2013), and Electoral Bond strike-down (2024).\n2. SC has actively filled legislative gaps in election law where Parliament has been inactive.\n3. SC's electoral reform orders are binding only until Parliament enacts contrary legislation.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct. The SC has driven major electoral reforms through judicial orders. Parliament can override many (not all — fundamental right aspects cannot be overridden) SC orders on electoral matters by legislation.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about ECI and caste/religion-based voter mobilisation:\n1. The SC in S.R. Bommai v. Union of India held that use of religion in elections is unconstitutional.\n2. The Constitution (24th Amendment) specifically banned caste-based political parties.\n3. The Hindutva Case (Dr. Ramesh Prabhoo v. Prabhakar Kunte 1996) held that seeking votes in the name of Hindutva is not a corrupt practice.\nWhich are correct?","options":["3 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect — S.R. Bommai dealt with President's Rule, not election corrupt practices. Statement 2 is incorrect — no such 24th Amendment provision exists. Statement 3 is the controversial Prabhoo ruling which held Hindutva was a 'way of life' not just religion.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about Section 8 of the RPA 1951 (disqualification for conviction):\n1. A person convicted of an offence with a sentence of 2 years or more is disqualified from contesting elections.\n2. The disqualification under Section 8 applies from the date of conviction, not the date sentence becomes final.\n3. A person convicted while an MP/MLA is not disqualified while appeal against conviction is pending.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — post Lily Thomas case (2013), the SC struck down Section 8(4) which earlier allowed MPs/MLAs to continue while appealing conviction. Now conviction itself causes disqualification. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the constitutional limit on ECI's executive powers:\n1. ECI cannot transfer civil servants (e.g., IAS officers) who are not election-related to break links of patronage.\n2. ECI can direct the transfer of District Collectors/SPs perceived as biased during elections.\n3. ECI can recommend action against bureaucrats who violate ECI directions, but cannot itself punish them.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect — ECI can and does transfer non-election civil servants (like IAS officers posted as DMs) if they are perceived as partisan. Statements 2 and 3 correctly describe ECI's scope of authority over bureaucrats.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the 'Schedule' of elections and its constitutional basis:\n1. ECI's power to fix the schedule of elections is derived from Article 324.\n2. There is no specific constitutional or statutory timeline within which ECI must announce elections after the House's term expires.\n3. ECI must conduct elections to the Lok Sabha within 6 months before its term expires.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is not a specific constitutional requirement — ECI determines the schedule, and the Constitution requires elections before the House's term ends (Article 83). The precise 6-month advance announcement is ECI practice, not a constitutional requirement. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about ECI's role in presidential elections:\n1. ECI conducts the Presidential election and counts votes.\n2. The President is elected by single transferable vote (STV) with proportional representation.\n3. Disputes regarding Presidential elections are decided by the Supreme Court.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct about Presidential elections: the ECI conducts them, STV with PR is used (Article 55), and disputes go to the SC (Article 71).","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about 'proxy voting' and special voter categories:\n1. Armed Forces personnel who cannot physically vote can designate a proxy to vote on their behalf.\n2. This proxy voting for service voters is provided under the Representation of the People Act.\n3. Postal ballot and proxy voting are available simultaneously — service voters can choose either.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct. Section 60(c) of the RPA and Conduct of Election Rules provide for proxy voting by service voters of the armed forces. They can choose between postal ballot and proxy voting.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about ECI's interaction with State Election Commissions (SECs):\n1. ECI and SECs are completely separate — ECI has no authority over SECs.\n2. CAPF deployed for Panchayat/Municipal elections is requested by the SEC, not ECI.\n3. ECI and SECs may coordinate on election schedules in states where state and Lok Sabha elections coincide.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct. ECI and SECs are constitutionally separate (Articles 243K/324). CAPFs for local body elections are sought by SECs. Coordination happens when elections coincide.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about financial accountability in elections and Section 29C disclosure:\n1. Section 29C of the RPA 1951 requires every political party to submit financial statements to ECI.\n2. Political parties that receive more than Rs 20,000 from a single donor must disclose the donor's name.\n3. All political party income (regardless of amount) must be disclosed to ECI under the 2023 amended rules.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — the threshold for mandatory name disclosure is currently Rs 20,000 (not all amounts). This threshold is the primary disclosure gateway. Statements 1 and 2 are correct under current RPA provisions.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about ECI and political funding transparency after Electoral Bonds case (2024):\n1. The SC directed SBI to disclose all electoral bond data to ECI.\n2. ECI was directed to publish the complete electoral bond donor-recipient data on its website.\n3. Following the SC order, ECI published the data showing which companies donated to which parties.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct — the SC directed SBI disclosure to ECI and public publication; this was a landmark transparency order. ECI did publish the data as directed.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about Section 126 of the RPA (prohibition on certain activities during election campaigns):\n1. Section 126 prohibits public meetings within 48 hours of polling closing.\n2. Social media campaigns are treated the same as public meetings under Section 126.\n3. ECI has specific guidelines extending Section 126 restrictions to digital/social media campaigning.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect — social media is not 'treated the same' as public meetings under Section 126 (the law was written pre-internet). ECI has issued separate digital media guidelines extending the silence period concept online. Statements 1 and 3 are correct.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the concept of 'One Nation One Election' and its constitutional implications:\n1. Implementing simultaneous elections would require amendments to Articles 83, 85, 172, and 174 at minimum.\n2. Simultaneous elections would require States to agree through constitutional amendment — it cannot be imposed by Parliament alone.\n3. The Kovind High-Level Committee (2024) recommended simultaneous elections.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct. Multiple constitutional articles need amendment. Federalism concerns suggest state concurrence is necessary. The Kovind committee (2023-24) recommended the move. All three are accurate.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about ECI's 'de-registration' powers over political parties:\n1. ECI has clear statutory powers to deregister political parties under Section 29A of the RPA.\n2. ECI has deregistered thousands of registered parties due to inactivity and non-filing of returns.\n3. A deregistered party can approach the HC challenging ECI's order.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect — Section 29A provides for registration but does NOT explicitly provide deregistration powers. ECI's deregistration is based on Article 324 plenary powers, not Section 29A expressly. Statements 2 and 3 are correct.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about ECI's order in the split of political parties (e.g., AIADMK, Shiv Sena, NCP):\n1. ECI determines which faction of a split party is the 'real' party entitled to the original name and symbol.\n2. ECI's test for the 'real' party includes majority in the legislature AND in the organisational wing.\n3. The decision of ECI on party splits under the Symbols Order is subject to judicial review.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct. ECI adjudicates party splits under the Symbols Order. The test includes both legislative and organisational majority (now primarily legislative per ECI practice). ECI orders are subject to HC/SC review.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about ECI's role in ensuring accessibility in elections:\n1. ECI is constitutionally mandated to ensure accessibility for persons with disabilities (PWDs) in elections.\n2. ECI has launched the 'Divyang' (PwD) friendly voting programme with ramps, braille on EVMs, and home voting for 85+ and PWDs.\n3. PWD accessibility in elections is a fundamental right enforceable against ECI under Article 21.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is imprecise — there is no specific constitutional mandate on ECI for PWD accessibility; ECI acts as a matter of policy and SC judgments interpreting Article 21. Statements 2 and 3 are correct.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about ECI's interpretive power vs. Parliamentary supremacy:\n1. ECI cannot override Acts of Parliament (like the RPA) through its directives.\n2. If Parliament passes a law that undermines free and fair elections, ECI can strike it down using Article 324.\n3. ECI can 'read down' or restrict application of Parliamentary laws that conflict with free elections under Article 324.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect — only courts can strike down unconstitutional laws; ECI cannot. Statement 3 is also incorrect — ECI cannot 'read down' Parliamentary laws; that is a judicial function. Statement 1 alone is correct.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about international election assistance and ECI:\n1. ECI provides technical assistance to developing democracies through the International Institute for Democracy and Electoral Assistance (IDEA).\n2. ECI conduct of elections is benchmarked by international bodies like UNDP and Commonwealth.\n3. India allows international election observers from the UN to monitor Indian elections.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — India does NOT allow international election observers (unlike many democratic countries). The government has maintained that Indian elections are a domestic matter. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the history of electoral fraud in India:\n1. Booth capturing, ballot stuffing, and rigging were major problems in early Indian elections.\n2. EVMs were introduced primarily to solve the problem of ballot paper stuffing.\n3. The introduction of EVMs has completely eliminated all forms of electoral malpractice in India.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — while EVMs eliminated paper ballot stuffing, other forms of malpractice (voter intimidation, impersonation, pre-poll violence, money/liquor distribution) continue. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about ECI's constitutional accountability:\n1. ECI does not submit annual reports to Parliament — unlike NHRC, CAG, or NCSC.\n2. Parliamentary committees can summon ECI officials to answer questions.\n3. ECI's actions can be questioned before Parliamentary standing committees.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 only","explanation":"Statements 2 and 3 are incorrect — ECI being a constitutional body independent of Parliament, Parliamentary committees generally do not summon ECI officials or scrutinise its operational decisions (though policy matters may be discussed). Statement 1 correctly notes ECI does not file parliamentary reports.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about disqualification of elected members under Article 102 and ECI:\n1. Article 102 lists grounds of disqualification for MPs (holding office of profit, unsound mind, insolvent, alien citizenship, etc.).\n2. ECI's opinion is sought by the President for disqualification under Article 103.\n3. ECI's opinion under Article 103 is binding on the President without exception.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct. Article 102 lists disqualification grounds. Article 103 requires the President to obtain ECI's opinion. The SC has held ECI's opinion is binding (Jyoti Basu case), though recent controversies have revisited this.","chapter_number":48,"level":3},
]
